==English==

===Etymology===
From an earlier {{m|en|tardive}}, from {{bor|en|fr|tardif}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|tardīvus}}, from {{der|en|la|tardus||slow”, “sluggish}}, of obscure origin.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɑːdi/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɑɹdi/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tardy.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɑː(ɹ)di|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# [[late|Late]]; [[overdue]] or [[delayed]].
#: {{ux|en|He yawned, then raised a '''tardy''' hand over his mouth.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|act=IV|scene=iii|passage=When everything is ended, then you come. / These '''tardy''' tricks of yours will, on my life, / One time or other break some gallows’ back.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1795|author={{w|Isaac D'Israeli|Isaac D’Israeli}}|title=An Essay on the Manners and Genius of the Literary Character|location=London|publisher=T. Cadell Jr. and W. Davies|chapter=9|page=122|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004871875.0001.000
|passage=Men of genius anticipate their contemporaries, and know they are such, long before the '''tardy''' consent of the public.}}
#* {{RQ:Saki Beasts|chapter=The Stake|pages=202–203|passage=As a matter of fact, the luncheon fare, when it made its '''tardy''' appearance, was distinctly unworthy of the reputation which the justly-treasured cook had built up for herself.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author=w:James Baldwin|chapter=Down at the Cross: Letter from a Region in My Mind|title=w:The Fire Next Time|location=New York|publisher=Dial|page=87|url=https://archive.org/details/firenexttime00bald
|passage=And the Black Muslims, along with many people who are not Muslims, no longer wish for a recognition so grudging and (should it ever be achieved) so '''tardy'''.}}
# Moving with a slow pace or motion; not swift.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|act=II|scene=i|passage={{...}} fashions in proud Italy, / Whose manners still our '''tardy''' apish nation / Limps after in base imitation.}}
#* {{RQ:Sandys Divine Poems|chapter=To the Prince|passage=Nor should their Age by Yeares be told: / Whose Souls, more swift then Motion, clime; / And check the '''tardy''' Flight of Time.}}
#* {{RQ:Prior Poems|Carmen Seculare, For the Year 1700. To the King|page=151|passage=In various Views she tries her constant Theme; / Finds him, in Councils, and in Arms, the same: / When certain to o’ercome, inclin’d to save; / '''Tardy''' to Vengeance; and with Mercy brave.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Nicholas Nickleby|chapter=XLIX|passage={{...}} a disease which medicine never cured, wealth never warded off, or poverty could boast exemption from; which sometimes moves in giant strides, and sometimes at a '''tardy''' sluggish pace, but, slow or quick, is ever sure and certain.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1926|author=w:Hope Mirrlees|title=w:Lud-in-the-Mist|publisher=Millennium|year_published=2000|chapter=19|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ludinthemist00mirr_0
|passage=These berries {{...}} are a deadly and insidious poison, though very '''tardy''' in their action, often lying dormant in the blood for many days.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1972|lyricist=Ian Anderson|title=w:Thick As A Brick|artist={{w|Jethro Tull (band)|Jethro Tull}}|passage=And the youngest of the family<br>Is moving with authority<br>Building castles by the sea<br>He dares the '''tardy''' tide<br>To wash them all aside.}}
# [[ineffectual|Ineffectual]]; slow-witted, slow to act, or [[dull]].
#: {{ux|en|His '''tardy''' performance bordered on incompetence.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[unwary|Unwary]]; [[unready]] (especially in the phrase ''take (someone) tardy'').
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|act=IV|scene=i|passage=Be not ta’en '''tardy''' by unwise delay.}}
#* {{RQ:Butler Hudibras|year=1905|canto=III|page=104|passage=Yield, ''Scoundrel'' base (quoth she) or die; / Thy life is mine, and liberty. / But if thou think’st I took thee '''tardy''', / And dar’st presume to be so hardy, / To try thy fortune o’re afresh, / I’le wave my title to thy flesh,}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[criminal|Criminal]]; [[guilty]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1697|author=w:Jeremy Collier|title=Essays upon Several Moral Subjects
|passage=And the Franks served the Men much the same {{long s}}auce when they found them '''tardy''', and made them run their Heats through the Streets}}

====Usage notes====
* The term suggests habitual lateness.
* Somewhat dated in the United Kingdom.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|late; overdue; not on time}} {{l|en|belated}}, {{l|en|delayed}}

